tually  published a paper yet talking about  their updated alpha fold system  it seems to be that this process is  iterative such that the information is  passed between these different  these different representations to then  finally generate  a matrix that gives the information to  form the final 3d structure  so how long does this process take well  if we go to a cell  it can fold a protein in a fraction of a  second so  fast and if we go to experimental models  such as  x-ray crystallography or nmr or query m  it can take months up to several years  to get a final 3d structure  deepmind's alpha folds can do it in a  couple of days  and it's not just fast compared to  experimental approaches  but it's also accurate they achieved a  score of 92.4  across all of their targets at casper 14  and so this score effectively takes into  account  how far each amino acid residue in their  predicted model  is from the actual experimental model so  does this mean that  structural biologists who generate  protein structures  using query m x-ray nmr are out of the  job  well not quite firstly alpha fold was  trained by using the publicly available  data  consisting of a of around 170 000  protein structures  that have been experimentally generated  so if they want to improve alpha fold  they need to be able to at least compare  it to some experimental data  and i initially thought that this might  be quite a weak argument  but especially for the validation it's  actually quite important  it's important to know if the structures  that they're predicting are actually  correct  and secondly x-ray crystallography and  chromium  just the two examples have seen massive  improvements over the last few years  and they can actually generate  information that can't be gained by  using alpha folds  for example you can now get chromium  structures of not just one protein but  entire protein complexes how one protein  is sandwiched  amongst other proteins and having a full  pitch of the entire complex  gives you so much more information than  just that protein individually  as many proteins function in a cell as a  complex  a second advantage to experimental  approaches is that you can get  the protein on different structures for  example going back to my favorite  protein p53  a decent number of structures have been  released that not only show p53 in its  tetrameric form  so it doesn't work alone it works with  four of its equal bodies  but it also includes dna in the  structure  so we can see how the protein is  interacting with the dna  and it's not just dna we can do it with  rna or other small molecules  using experimental systems and so far  alpha folds i don't think it can  actually do any of that  it's just getting the structure of a  protein based on one  individual amino acid sequence and  another advantage to experimental  approaches such as using nmr  nuclear magnetic resonance you can  sometimes get  quite an insight into the dynamics of a  protein  because proteins i mean we draw them as  like static entities  but in a cell that all you know did all  sorts of kind of  conformational changes and that's also  really important for a protein function  but yes there are many advantages to  alpha folds  not only the speed as i mentioned  earlier it's kind of crazy how fast it  is in comparison to  some of the x-ray structures but it's  also useful for  being able to generate at least  predicted  structures for proteins that previously  were really challenging  to try and crystallize because i mean  the reason x-ray crystallography  aquarium takes so long is because it's  so hard to get the right experimental  conditions  in which a protein may actually  crystallize and you may never find that  condition  some proteins may never crystallize  which is why having something like alpha  folds where it can predict  you know we'll never know how accurate  it is or maybe not as accurate but it's  better than having no structure at all  and so i think this nicely brings me on  to what are the uses of having protein  structures  and i'm actually going to kind of split  this into two firstly  talk about the use of having protein  folding structure  oh man having protein folding predicting  systems the advantages of those and what  we can use with that information  and then we're generally looking at what  we can gain from a protein structure  so let's start with the use of having  something like alpha folds  well what's really interesting about  alpha fold is that  it collects all this different  information about amino acid sequences  and brings it all together to generate a  3d structure  and now one thing that would be really  interesting would be to kind of  reverse this process and work backwards  to try and understand  what information it was using what was  more important to generate  the 3d structure of prediction as that  might give us some information  into how the folding mechanism actually  works within a cell  because that you know understanding the  folding mechanism is one of the  aspects that goes along with trying to  crack the protein folding problem  and if this could be understood it may  also then be possible to  create your own protein structure and  then to use the reverse mechanism to  kind of see what the original protein  sequence would be  but i think i'm a little bit ahead of  myself what are the actual more  practical uses of  alpha folds well firstly having protein  structures gives us insight into the  protein's function  and that's just useful for understanding  what that cell's doing  and understanding what's going on within  each of our cells  and by being more specific there's a  variety of things that we can get with a  protein structure  firstly it gives us an understanding of  interactions for example with different  drugs and so this is really useful for  drug discovery  and therapeutics and then secondly a lot  of diseases are caused by genetic  mutations  within within different genes and those  genetic mutations can often  alter the amino acid present within a  protein and so by having this predictive  system we can see how the protein  changes  when that amino acid is changed and try  to understand  the disease mechanism and similarly with  this  actually kind of a bit not quite related  um  are diseases such as alzheimer's disease  and type 2 diabetes were it's often seen  that proteins  aggregate and understanding the  aggregation process can be greatly aided  by having the structures of these  different proteins  for example the tau protein which is the  protein that that  can aggregate in alzheimer's disease was  only recently solved by chronium  but it's one of those proteins that was  really challenging to study and so  something like alpha folds is a really  great tool  and similarly loss of proteostasis  happens in the aging process  so understanding how protein structures  change during age could also  be aided through alpha folds but my  point is there's a lot of applications  of using something like alpha folds  i mean there wouldn't be the casper  competition to begin with if it wasn't  going to be important  and so hopefully this video has given  you some insights into protein folding  into the alpha fold system and into the  potential applications  and so maybe now we can understand this  quote that it will change  everything which i used as a little bit  of clickbait for this video  i guess i'll see you later if it  actually worked um anyway so  as always i hope we've learned something  in this video um i actually really  enjoyed making this one  i got proper nerdy and into it but i  hope you've learned something  and as always thanks for listening
 

